compress-utils: 64-bit registers for zstd on wasm32.

Applied to third_party/zstd (v1.5.7) by the WASM build only, never to the vendored tree itself (see third_party/VENDOR.md). Inert unless ZSTD_WASM_WIDE_REGS is defined on a wasm32 target (cmake sets it for the zstd objects of the WASM build); ZSTD_FORCE_WIDE_REGS enables it on any target and is test-only.

wasm32 has 32-bit pointers but native 64-bit integer registers. zstd keys its register-width decisions on sizeof(size_t), so on wasm32 it takes the 32-bit bit-container and reload paths. This patch introduces MEM_regT (U64 when enabled, size_t otherwise) and uses it for:

  - the bit container and its reload decisions in the decoder (bitstream.h, huf_decompress.c, zstd_decompress_block.c)
  - the matching flush decisions in the encoder (zstd_compress.c, zstd_compress_sequences.c)
  - the SWAR tag mask of the row-based match finder (zstd_lazy.c)

Address-width checks (MEM_32bits() / MEM_64bits()) keep meaning address width, and the fast 4-stream Huffman loop stays disabled (it assumes 64-bit pointers). When the macros are undefined the new names are plain aliases of the size_t-keyed ones.

Compressed output is byte-identical with and without the patch.

